explanation:
This is an auxiliary notion made up for the proof. The module docstring says it is not in the literature and was introduced only to formalize Rosenlicht's proof of Liouville's theorem. The file uses it as a scaffold:
- it holds for F over itself (`IsLiouville.rfl`);
- it is transitive (`IsLiouville.trans`);
- it passes to finite-dimensional intermediate fields and along algebra equivalences;
- finite-dimensional extensions in characteristic 0 satisfy it (`isLiouville_of_finiteDimensional`).

So there is no standard textbook object to compare it with.

The body itself is coherent and suits that purpose. A finite family of constants c taken in F, elements u and v of K, and the identity a = ∑ cᵢ·logDeriv uᵢ + v′ together imply the existence of such a representation with everything in F. The body differs slightly from a literal reading of the docstring, which says "v, cᵢ, uᵢ ∈ K and cᵢ constant". The body requires the cᵢ to lie in F (constants of F), not just to be constants of K.

This difference matters, and the body's choice is the right one. With cᵢ allowed to be constants of K, take ℂ(x)/ℝ(x). There 1/(x²+1) = (1/2i)(logDeriv(x−i) − logDeriv(x+i)), but 1/(x²+1) has no such representation over ℝ(x). The stronger version would therefore fail for this finite-dimensional extension and contradict `isLiouville_of_finiteDimensional`. The docstring is just loosely worded: it treats F as a subset of K and does not say where the constants live. The definition matches what the library needs, but it is a formalization device rather than a standard mathematical object.
